  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat Advisory and a potential for a few strong \nto severe storms Saturday. \n\nHigh pressure aloft will support high temperatures in the mid \nto upper 90s. The heat combined with high relative humidity will\nyield Heat Indices of 104-109F with a few higher spots not out \nof the question. As a result, plan to issue another forecast \narea-wide Heat Advisory for Saturday from around 11a-8p. This \nstrong heating in a high PWAT environment with a nearby weak \nfront along with the seabreeze front will likely lead to \nscattered storms. Some of these storms could become strong to \nsevere in the afternoon/early evening but the shear is not all \nthat significant potentially marginalizing chances a bit across \nthe forecast area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous heat is expected through much of the \nupcoming week.\n\nBy Sunday the mid/upper ridge will become focused across the \nlower MS Valley but it will continue to influence weather \nconditions across the area. The heat combined with high relative\nhumidity will continue to support high Heat Indices across the \narea. The worst of the conditions will likely be Monday and \nTuesday afternoon with a Heat Index potentially 110+ for a \nlarger extent. Since the forecast area is on the eastern side of\nthe ridge during this time, the W- NWly flow aloft could lead \nto some hard to time impulses being directed across the forecast\narea. These storms could provide relief for some areas this \nweek, with lesser storm chances Monday and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Minor coastal flooding is likely into early \nnext week along the lower Cape Fear River.\n\nHigh astronomical tides and continued tidal anomalies will lead\nto minor coastal flooding along the lower Cape Fear River with \nhigh tide tonight. These anomalies will persist into early next \nweek, with the best chances for minor coastal flooding along the\nlower Cape Fear River with the nighttime high tide.",
